---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const phonologyCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Phoneme: the sound that makes meaning', note: 'In English, [p] and [b] distinguish pat/bat — separate phonemes. In Arabic, [p]/[b] are one phoneme (the same word). Phonemes are defined per language: the minimal meaning-distinguishing sound units.' },
    { math: '2 · Allophones: the costume changes', note: 'The same phoneme varies by position: English [p] is aspirated (puffed) in "pin", unaspirated in "spin" — heard as the same sound by English speakers, as different sounds by Thai speakers. Allophones are the costume; the phoneme is the character.' },
    { math: '3 · Rules are systematic', note: 'English plural: /s/ after voiceless sounds (cats), /z/ after voiced (dogs), /ɪz/ after sibilants (buses). Not memorised per word — a rule applies across the lexicon, including nonsense words (you know "wugs" pluralises with /z/ — the wug test).' },
    { math: '4 · Loanwords expose the system', note: 'Japanese adds a vowel to consonant clusters ("sutoraiku" for strike) because its phonology forbids clusters. Borrowed words get forced through the borrower\'s sound system — phonology is the immigration officer.' },
  ],
};

const phonologyQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    { q: 'The diagnostic for "these two sounds are separate phonemes" is…', choices: ['they sound different', 'a minimal pair exists', 'they have different spellings', 'one is harder to say'], answer: 1, why: 'A minimal pair (pin/bin) differs in one sound and one meaning — proving that sound difference alone can carry meaning in that language.' },
    { q: 'English [pʰ] in "pin" and [p] in "spin" are…', choices: ['separate phonemes', 'allophones of one phoneme', 'a spelling difference', 'different words'], answer: 1, why: 'The aspiration difference is predictable from position (word-initial before a stressed vowel), carries no meaning, and English speakers hear one sound — the allophone signature.' },
    { q: 'A child who says "two wugs" for a made-up creature has demonstrated…', choices: ['imitation', 'a stored list of plurals', 'an internal rule of plural formation', 'spelling knowledge'], answer: 2, why: 'Berko\'s 1958 wug test: the child never heard "wug" before, so the plural must be generated by rule — with /z/ after the voiced final g, exactly as in "dogs".' },
    { q: 'Japanese "sutoraiku" for English "strike" is an example of…', choices: ['translation', 'loanword adaptation to phonotactics', 'vowel shift', 'assimilation of meaning'], answer: 1, why: 'Japanese syllables are (mostly) consonant-plus-vowel, so forbidden clusters get repaired with epenthetic vowels — the borrower\'s sound system processes the loan.' },
  ],
};

const poRelBuckets = {
  "prompt": "An inventory is a fact about a language — file each case:",
  "buckets": [
    "contrastive in English — separate phonemes",
    "merged in English — one phoneme here, split or used elsewhere"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "sip/zip, cat/bat, light/right — swap the sound, swap the meaning",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "Hindi's dental versus retroflex t — two phonemes there; English hears one",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "p versus b for Arabic speakers — borrowings with p come out as b, and the difference sounds alien rather than meaningful",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "Thai's aspirated p — contrastive there; English treats both p-sounds as one",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "About 24 consonants and around 20 vowels — roughly 44, a middling count: the inventory is about the language's organisation, not the sounds themselves."
};

const poAlloMatch = {
  "prompt": "The phoneme is the character; allophones are the costumes — pair them:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "/p/ at the start of a stressed syllable ('pin', 'attack')",
      "right": "aspirated [pʰ]"
    },
    {
      "left": "/p/ after s ('spin', 'suspend')",
      "right": "unaspirated [p]"
    },
    {
      "left": "/l/ before vowels ('leaf')",
      "right": "clear l — front of tongue raised"
    },
    {
      "left": "/l/ at word ends ('feel')",
      "right": "dark l — back of tongue raised"
    }
  ],
  "why": "The aspiration rule is exceptionless across the lexicon and applied every second — yet no speaker can state it."
};

const poDiagnoseBranch = {
  "root": "Fieldwork: an unwritten language, two sounds on tape. What are they to each other?",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Swapping one sound for the other sometimes changes a word's meaning — unpredictably. Verdict?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Contrastive — separate phonemes",
          "next": "phoneme",
          "feedback": "Meaning-differences that resist prediction are the phoneme signature."
        },
        {
          "label": "The difference tracks position — one only ever appears before a stressed vowel",
          "next": "allophone",
          "feedback": "Predictable from position: allophones."
        }
      ]
    },
    "phoneme": {
      "text": "Separate phonemes — the contrast lives in the inventory. But the two sounds are acoustically very similar; does that matter?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Ask what the ear alone can decide",
          "next": "ear"
        }
      ]
    },
    "allophone": {
      "text": "Allophones — the costume, not the character. A learner's native system will transfer: producing these where the target language has phonemes.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "But no minimal pair exists for them anywhere — now what?",
          "next": "fieldwork"
        }
      ]
    },
    "ear": {
      "text": "Acoustic distance misleads: clear and dark l differ dramatically yet are allophones, while Hindi's dental and retroflex t are acoustically close and are separate phonemes. Contrast, not similarity, decides.",
      "choices": []
    },
    "fieldwork": {
      "text": "Most contrasts in most languages lack a perfect pair — fall back on patterned distribution: patient word lists, and the wug test run in reverse on an adult system.",
      "choices": []
    }
  },
  "why": "Contrast versus predictability decides; the ear alone misleads — and accent transfer falls straight out of it: allophones where the target language has phonemes."
};

const poPluralSpot = {
  "prompt": "One line of this plural report is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "After voiceless sounds the ending is [s]: cats, books.",
    "After voiced sounds it is [z]: dogs, pens.",
    "After sibilants it is [ɪz]: buses, judges.",
    "You memorise the ending word by word.",
    "Children overapply the rule ('foots', 'goed') before settling on the exceptions."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "Nobody memorised it per word — the rule assigns by the final sound across the whole lexicon, nonsense words included ('the plural of blork is blorks').",
  "why": "Overapplication is the fingerprint of a rule at work — the same logic the wug test made famous."
};

const poHawaiiEstimate = {
  "prompt": "Japanese syllables are almost strictly consonant-plus-vowel; English permits three-consonant onsets ('strength') and a four-consonant coda (/strɛŋkθs/). How many consonants does Hawaiian's entire phonology run?",
  "unit": "consonants",
  "answer": 8,
  "factor": 1.4,
  "why": "Eight consonants and five vowels, with syllables that never end in a consonant — legality lines each language draws for itself, which is why 'bnick' sounds impossible and 'Klaxon' feels native.",
  "context": "Phonotactics can be stated precisely enough to program — and loanwords get forced through it ('sutoraiku' for strike)."
};

const poConnectedMatch = {
  "prompt": "Rule-governed interactions — pair the process with its example:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "'input' sounding like 'imput'",
      "right": "assimilation — the sound copies its neighbour's features"
    },
    {
      "left": "'camera' to 'camra', 'chocolate' to \"choc'late\"",
      "right": "deletion — sounds vanish in fast speech, predictably"
    },
    {
      "left": "'hamster' heard as 'hampster'",
      "right": "epenthesis — an insertion repairs an illegal sequence"
    },
    {
      "left": "written transcriptions of real speech always look wrong",
      "right": "the ear cleans up what the mouth did — perception runs through the phoneme layer"
    }
  ],
  "why": "All three run automatically, below awareness — evidence that perception is organised, not raw."
};

const poAccentCompare = {
  "prompt": "Rhoticity — the same grammar, different settings — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "Rhotic — most American and Irish accents",
    "text": "'Car' keeps its r-sound before consonants — one rule, switched on."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "Non-rhotic — most English and Australian accents",
    "text": "The r is deleted before consonants — the same rule, switched off. Mergers ('cot'/'caught' collapsing into one vowel, spreading right now) and th-fronting ('fink' for 'think') are more settings, not sloppiness."
  },
  "why": "Because rules are systematic, accents are mutually intelligible systems with predictable maps of difference — the same machinery that ran the Great Vowel Shift, rule by rule."
};

const poStressJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "'INsult' the noun versus 'inSULT' the verb — stress alone fixes the meaning.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Nouns and verbs borrowed as pairs swap stress; no letters change."
    },
    {
      "s": "Vowels reduce to schwa when unstressed — 'photograph' versus 'photography' has the same letters and different vowels.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The stress moved, so the vowels followed."
    },
    {
      "s": "English is syllable-timed: every syllable gets an even slice of time.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "English is stress-timed — stressed syllables land at roughly even intervals while unstressed ones crowd between."
    },
    {
      "s": "The aspiration of 'pin' appears word-initially before a stressed vowel — stress conditions the allophones.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The rule's environment is stated in stress terms."
    },
    {
      "s": "Second-language learners rarely need to add English stress consciously.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "It is the feature learners most often have to add consciously — the rhythm they hear as the language's music."
    }
  ]
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/intermediate/phonology">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    Two lessons supply what this one assumes. The foundational Linguistics lesson, How
    language works, established that speakers know rules they cannot state; and the
    intermediate lesson Phonetics and the IPA supplied the sounds themselves. Phonology now
    asks the next question: which sound differences does a given language treat as
    meaningful, and which are just predictable variants?
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define the phoneme through minimal pairs, and say why inventories are language-relative;</li>
    <li>separate allophones from phonemes using predictability versus contrast;</li>
    <li>state the English plural rule and explain what the wug test proved;</li>
    <li>read a language's phonotactics off loanword adaptations like "sutoraiku";</li>
    <li>describe accents — rhoticity, mergers, th-fronting — as rule settings rather than mouth failures.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Phonetics gave you the sounds; phonology gives you the <strong>system</strong>. Two
    languages can share physical sounds and use them differently: English and Thai both
    produce aspirated p, but only Thai lets it distinguish words. Phonology is the study of
    that organisation — <strong>which sound differences carry meaning</strong> (phonemes),
    <strong>which are predictable variants</strong> (allophones), and the <strong>rules</strong>
    that govern where each appears. It is the first place in linguistics where you can prove
    that speakers know an invisible structure: every rule in this lesson is one you have
    never been taught and never miss.
  </p>
  <p>
    The payoff is twofold. Scientifically, phonology is where the "speaker knows grammar
    they cannot state" argument begins — it is the cleanest laboratory for tacit knowledge.
    Practically, it explains a remarkable amount of daily experience: foreign accents (which
    are phonology, not physiology), spelling systems, why some wordplay works and some
    falls flat, and why borrowed words come out the shape they do.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="phonemes, allophones, rules, and what loanwords reveal" widget="equationStep" config={phonologyCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four cards and watch each add a layer
    — contrast (phonemes), positional variation (allophones), productivity (rules), and
    what borrowed words reveal about the whole system. Then check card 2 against your own
    speech: say "pin" and "spin" with a hand in front of your mouth and feel the aspiration
    you have been producing all your life without knowing it.
  </p>

  <h2>The phoneme: contrast that matters</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>phoneme</strong> is a sound category that can distinguish words. The test is
    the <strong>minimal pair</strong>: two words differing in exactly one sound with
    different meanings — pin/bin, sip/zip, cat/bat, light/right. If swapping the sound swaps
    the meaning, the two sounds are separate phonemes in that language. The definition is
    deliberately language-relative: Arabic has no p/b contrast (borrowings with p are
    pronounced b, and Arabic speakers hear the difference as alien, not meaningful), while
    Hindi splits English t into two phonemes (dental versus retroflex) that English merges.
    An inventory is therefore a fact about a language, not about the sounds. English runs
    about 24 consonant phonemes and (by accent) somewhere around 20 vowels and diphthongs —
    roughly 44 in total, a middling number by world standards.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="same two sounds, different languages, different fates" widget="sort-buckets" config={poRelBuckets} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> phonemes are not the same as letters. "Sh" is one
  phoneme written with two letters; "x" in "box" is two phonemes (k, s) in one letter;
  and rhotic accents have more vowel contrasts than non-rhotic ones. The phoneme lives in
  the mouth and ear, not the alphabet.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a phoneme is defined by contrast — minimal pairs decide — and
    an inventory is a fact about a language, not about the sounds. But not every audible
    difference earns a place in the inventory, which is where allophones enter.
  </p>

  <h2>Allophones: the costume changes</h2>
  <p>
    The same phoneme is pronounced differently depending on its environment, and the
    variants — <strong>allophones</strong> — are systematic, not random. The classic case:
    English /p/ is aspirated [pʰ] at the start of a stressed syllable ("pin", "attack") and
    unaspirated [p] after s ("spin", "suspend"). The rule is exceptionless across the
    lexicon; English speakers apply it every second and cannot state it. Speakers of Thai,
    where aspiration is contrastive, hear the two p-sounds easily; English speakers hear
    "the same sound" because in English the difference is never meaningful. Another
    standard case: English /l/ is "clear" (front of tongue raised) before vowels ("leaf")
    and "dark" (back of tongue raised) at word ends ("feel") — a difference so sharp that
    some languages use the two l-sounds as separate phonemes. Allophones are the costume;
    the phoneme is the character.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each environment with the costume it gets" widget="match" config={poAlloMatch} />
  <p>
    The diagnostic, formalised: sounds are separate phonemes if the difference is
    <strong>contrastive</strong> (creates meaning differences, unpredictably) and
    allophones if it is <strong>predictable</strong> from position. This single distinction
    explains most of what foreign accents are: a speaker transfers their native system —
    producing allophones where the target language has phonemes (the French r of an English
    learner) or missing contrasts the target language requires (the Japanese r/l merger).
    Accents are phonologies colliding, not mouths failing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="diagnose two sounds — walk the decision" widget="branch" config={poDiagnoseBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> sounds are separate phonemes when they sound very different, and allophones when they sound similar. The diagnostic is contrast, not acoustic distance: English's clear and dark l differ dramatically yet are allophones, while Hindi's dental and retroflex t are acoustically close and are separate phonemes. Predictability versus contrast decides; the ear alone misleads.</p>

  <h2>Rules are real: the plural and the wug test</h2>
  <p>
    Take one piece of English grammar and look at how you actually do it. The plural -s
    has three pronunciations, assigned by a rule: [s] after voiceless sounds (cats, books),
    [z] after voiced sounds (dogs, pens), [ɪz] after sibilants (buses, judges). Nobody
    memorised this per word — you pluralise new words instantly and correctly ("the plural
    of 'blork' is 'blorks'"), and children acquiring English apply the rule and then
    overapply it ("foots", "goed") before settling on the exceptions — overapplication is
    the fingerprint of a rule at work.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="find the line the rule refuses" widget="spot-error" config={poPluralSpot} />
  <p>
    The experimental proof is the <strong>wug test</strong> (Jean Berko Gleason, 1958):
    children shown a drawing of an imaginary creature — "This is a wug" — and shown two of
    them — "Now there are two ___" — produced "wugs" with the correct voiced [z], a form
    they had never heard. Children a year or two older handled plurals with sibilants
    ("nizzes"-style forms) the rule predicts. Since the word was invented on the spot, the
    plural cannot have been memorised: the child possesses a <strong>rule</strong>, applied
    productively to anything the lexicon will ever contain. The wug test is the founding
    demonstration of internalised grammar, and it works in phonology's favour across
    dozens of constructions since.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the plural's three pronunciations are assigned by a rule that
    even nonsense words obey, and the wug test showed children generating forms they had
    never heard. Rules also decide which sequences of sounds are legal at all — the
    subject of the next section.
  </p>

  <h2>Phonotactics: the rules of possible words</h2>
  <p>
    Every language restricts which sound sequences are legal, and speakers know the
    restrictions without instruction. "Blick" sounds like a possible English word; "bnick"
    does not; "ptak" sounds foreign (though "ptarmigan" sneaks through from Gaelic —
    spelling misleading again). The syllable has internal structure — <strong>onset,
    nucleus, coda</strong> — and each language draws its own legality lines: Japanese
    syllables are almost strictly consonant-plus-vowel (n allowed at the end); English
    permits three-consonant onsets ("strength") and a four-consonant coda (that same word,
    /strɛŋkθs/ — count them: ŋ, k, θ, s); Hawaiian's entire phonology is eight consonants
    and five vowels with syllables never ending in consonants. Phonotactics is why
    brand-names feel native ("Klaxon" yes, "Mxlot" no) and why the constraints can be
    stated precisely enough to program.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call Hawaiian's whole inventory" widget="estimate" config={poHawaiiEstimate} />

  <h2>Processes in connected speech</h2>
  <p>
    In fluent speech, sounds interact — and the interactions are rule-governed too.
    <strong>Assimilation</strong>: a sound copies features of its neighbour ("input"
    becoming "imput", n softening to m before p; "ten bikes" sounding like "tem bikes").
    <strong>Deletion</strong>: whole sounds vanish in fast speech ("camera" to "camra",
    "chocolate" to "choc'late") in patterns that are regular enough to predict.
    <strong>Epenthesis</strong>: sounds get inserted to repair illegal sequences ("hamster"
    heard as "hampster", a p inserted between m and s). These processes run automatically,
    below awareness, and they are the reason written transcriptions of real speech always
    look wrong: the ear cleans up what the mouth actually did, which is itself evidence
    that perception runs through the phoneme layer, not the raw phonetic one.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each process with its giveaway" widget="match" config={poConnectedMatch} />

  <h2>Accents as rule systems: the same grammar, different settings</h2>
  <p>
    Accent differences are phonological rules set differently, and they can be stated
    precisely. <strong>Rhoticity</strong>: most American and Irish accents pronounce r
    before consonants ("car" with an r-sound); most English and Australian accents delete
    it — one rule, switched on or off. <strong>Mergers</strong>: many North American
    accents have merged "cot" and "caught" into one vowel — two phonemes collapsed into
    one, a change spreading geographically right now and observable in real time.
    <strong>Th-fronting</strong>: some British accents say "fink" for "think", replacing
    one phoneme's realisation with another's. Each is a rule difference, not sloppiness —
    and because rules are systematic, accents are mutually intelligible systems with
    predictable maps of difference. Historically the same machinery runs: sound change
    proceeds rule by rule (the Great Vowel Shift was a cascade of them), which is why
    related languages share regular correspondences rather than random look-alikes — the
    comparative method in historical linguistics is built directly on phonology's
    regularity.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drag the divider — one rule, two settings" widget="compare" config={poAccentCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a foreign accent is a mouth problem — the tongue is "too thick" for the new sounds. Accents are phonologies colliding: the speaker transfers their native system, producing allophones where the target language has phonemes and missing the contrasts it requires. The anatomy is fine; the settings are wrong — which is why adult learners can retrain once they learn to hear the missing contrast.</p>

  <h2>Stress: the hidden organiser</h2>
  <p>
    Above the segment, <strong>word stress</strong> organises English phonology more than
    anything visible in spelling. Stress placement is partly rule-governed ("café" but
    "coffee"; "-ity" words stress the syllable before the suffix — "electricity"; nouns
    and verbs borrowed as pairs swap stress — "INsult" the noun versus "inSULT" the
    verb), and stress conditions the allophones: the aspiration of "pin" appears word-initially before a
    stressed vowel, and vowels reduce to schwa when unstressed ("photograph" /
ˈfoʊtəɡrɑːf/ versus "photography" /fəˈtɑːɡrəfi/ — the same letters, different vowels,
    because the stress moved). English is <strong>stress-timed</strong>: stressed syllables
    land at roughly even intervals while unstressed syllables crowd between them —
    the rhythm native speakers hear as the language's music, and the feature second-
    language learners most often have to add consciously.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="true or false — the hidden organiser" widget="judge" config={poStressJudge} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In a language, swapping the sound t for d never changes a word's meaning. Are they phonemes or allophones there, and what would confirm it?</li>
    <li>What exactly did a child who answers "two wugs" demonstrate?</li>
    <li>Japanese renders English "strike" as "sutoraiku". What does that adaptation reveal about Japanese phonology?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Allophones, most likely — no contrast means no meaning
    difference. Confirm with distribution: if t and d each appear only in predictable
    positions they are allophones; if both appear unpredictably in the same environment,
    they are separate phonemes. (2) An internal rule of plural formation: the child had
    never heard "wug", so the voiced z-sound must have been generated by rule from the
    voiced final g — the wug test's founding demonstration. (3) That its phonotactics
    forbid consonant clusters: Japanese syllables are almost strictly consonant-plus-vowel,
    so borrowed words get repaired with epenthetic vowels — the borrower's sound system
    processes the loan.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A phoneme is a sound category that can distinguish words; the minimal pair is the test.</li>
    <li>Inventories are language-relative: Arabic merges p and b; Hindi splits English t in two.</li>
    <li>Allophones are predictable variants — contrast versus predictability is the diagnostic.</li>
    <li>Rules apply across the lexicon; the wug test proved children generate, not memorise.</li>
    <li>Phonotactics defines possible words, and loanwords are forced through the borrower's system.</li>
    <li>Assimilation, deletion and epenthesis run automatically in connected speech, below awareness.</li>
    <li>Accents are rule settings, not mouth failures — and sign languages have phonology without sound.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Morphology takes the same rule-bound, evidence-from-nonsense-words approach to
    word-building, and Syntax: trees and rules scales it to sentences — the wug test's
    logic returning at every level. Semantics and pragmatics then completes the linguistics
    ladder with meaning.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The modern treatment of all this is <strong>features and natural classes</strong>.
    Rules do not target arbitrary sounds; they target classes defined by the phonetic
    coordinates from the last lesson — all voiceless stops, all sibilants, all nasals —
    and stating rules over features ([±voice], [±continuant], [±nasal]) collapses hundreds
    of seemingly separate facts into a small system. This is also why the plural rule is
    really three sub-rules over one natural class: the sibilants get an extra syllable
    because the tongue physically cannot say s right after s. From there the field's
    theories diverge: classical generative phonology wrote rules in order; <strong>optimality
    theory</strong> (1993) replaced rule-ordering with ranked, violable constraints — the
    grammar scores candidate pronunciations, and the least-violating one wins — which
    handles typology (why languages differ) elegantly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two quantitative notes finish the picture. Inventories have asymmetries worth knowing:
    no language has more vowel qualities than consonant ones (most languages have five or
    six vowels; the smallest documented systems run three, the largest above a dozen),
    and every language's phonemes partition a continuous acoustic space — the boundaries
    are drawn by the system, and perception experiments show listeners hear through the
    category (categorical perception). And tone systems stratify: many African languages
    use two or three tones; Asian tone systems can run to six or more with contours;
    and some languages layer tone on top of stress — one of the places phonology shades
    into the prosody that organises whole sentences, which is as far as sounds can be
    pushed before they become grammar.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two closing evidence points push the lesson's thesis to its limit.
    <strong>Acquisition, step by step</strong>: infants are born discriminating the
    contrasts of every language (the head-turn experiments of the 1970s onward), then
    lose the unused ones between roughly six and twelve months — perceptual narrowing
    toward their own inventory — and their early word pronunciations obey emerging
    systematic rules (consonant harmony, cluster simplification) before any word list
    could explain them. The phoneme, in other words, is built during the first year of
    life, without instruction. And <strong>signed languages have phonology with no
    sound</strong>: sign phonemes are handshape, location, movement and orientation,
    with minimal pairs (signs differing in one parameter only), sign-order constraints
    playing the role of phonotactics, and "slips of the hand" that respect the system —
    the strongest single proof that what this lesson studied was never really about
    sound. Phonology is the organisation of <em>any</em> articulatory-perceptual system
    into contrast; speech just happened to be the first case.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> sign languages are mime, or gesture without grammar. They have full phonologies built from handshape, location and movement, with minimal pairs and rule-governed "slips of the hand" — the strongest proof that phonology was never really about sound, only about contrast.</p>
  <p>
    One methodological note belongs on the expert shelf: how phoneme inventories are
    actually established for a language nobody has studied. Field phoneticians elicit
    candidate words, hunt minimal pairs where they exist, and where pairs do not exist
    (most contrasts in most languages lack a perfect pair) fall back on the patterned
    distribution: if [p] and [pʰ] never contrast and one only ever appears before a
    stressed vowel, they are allophones; if both appear unpredictably in the same
    environment, they are phonemes. The procedure is the wug test run in reverse —
    probing an adult system instead of a child's — and it is why every claim in this
    lesson, however clean it looks, rests on patiently collected word lists. The
    inventories in the reference grammars you will meet are, almost always, somebody's
    fieldwork.
  </p>
